<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815)</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1815 CE · capital Naples</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Napoleonic kingdom of southern Italy under Joseph Bonaparte and Joachim Murat, which abolished feudalism in the south.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples</a> joined the coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sent an army in 1806. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">King Ferdinand IV</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under British protection, and Napoleon made his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Joseph abolished feudalism in 1806, closed monasteries and introduced French administration. In 1808 he became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> brother-in-law, who introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, built roads and fought brigandage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. The kingdom never controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> held with British help.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Murat</a> abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1814, but in 1815 he declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Rimini</a> Proclamation calling for Italian independence. Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolentino">Tolentino</a> in May 1815, he fled; landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> in October, he was captured and shot. Ferdinand returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_N%C3%A1poles_-_Trast%C3%A1mara.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naples</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph Bonaparte, Joachim Murat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan, Italian, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Napoleonic Kingdom of Naples, Murat's Naples</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815" aria-label="Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5029 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5349 976L5349 976M5349 976L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Naples. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
